{
  "about": "Sleevesight genre vocabulary: the tags that become genre labels (design/design.md, Genre labels). First taken from GENRE_FAMILIES in design/simulator.html, whose families, each with a hue, were dropped in #1020: nothing read them, since #439 made the chips plain and the genre cards they were kept for were never built. The tags keep the families' order, a line each: folk, rock, pop, hip-hop, soul, reggae, electronic, score, jazz and blues, world. labels overrides the default label, the tag in title case. synonyms groups spellings of one genre beyond punctuation and spacing, which give one label. musicbrainz gives each tag the MusicBrainz genre (CC0) through which its definition is found: the genre's Wikidata item and its English Wikipedia article give the definition a genre's panel shows (#523). A tag whose name, folded to its letters and digits, is one of MusicBrainz's genres has that genre (matched against MusicBrainz's genre list on 2026-10-27, and for the tags added in #1011 on 2026-11-06); a tag that is not one, or whose genre has no English article, has the nearest genre that has one, where one fits (#663, checked 2026-10-27): alternative and indie are alternative rock and indie rock, as Last.fm's listeners use them; goth and gothic are gothic rock; metal is heavy metal; punk is punk rock; chanson francaise is chanson française; rap is hip hop; neoclassical is neoclassical new age, the contemporary piano and strings Last.fm's listeners tag so; modern classical is contemporary classical, and contemporary country is country (#1011, checked 2026-11-06). wikipedia gives a tag its English Wikipedia article by title, for a tag with no MusicBrainz genre that fits or whose genre has no English article, where an article says what the tag means (#852, checked 2026-11-02): acoustic is Acoustic music, score Film score, choral Choir, orchestral Orchestra, world music World music (#1011, checked 2026-11-06); a tag with one is defined by it, not through MusicBrainz. #1011 (2026-11-06) widened the list with most of the genres MusicBrainz's tags use most that it dropped (the genre cloud of MusicBrainz's 200 most used genre tags, read on 2026-11-06; not those that are no style of music, such as non-music, comedy, ballad or concerto, nor the loosest, progressive and club), the genres of the cases seen on the phone (psychedelic rock, neo-psychedelia, Anatolian rock, which turkish psychedelia is a synonym of, afrobeat, world music), and a few of the common genres of other regions; and a family, world (dropped since, with the others, #1020), for music named by its region rather than by a style of the other families (Latin, Brazilian, African, Iberian, Turkish folk music). #1098 (2026-11-14) widened it again for the genres Wikidata states (P136), which become labels only when the list holds them: the music styles among the genres of a sample of Wikidata's items with a MusicBrainz work or artist id that the list lacked, each with its MusicBrainz genre (matched against MusicBrainz's genre list on 2026-11-14, each genre's Wikidata item checked to have an English article), chanson with its article (MusicBrainz has only chanson française), and rhythm and blues, electronic dance music and film score as spellings of r&b, edm and score; not popular music (too loose), forms such as symphony, ballad and march, nor rapping, a technique. The tags left without either are listed, each with why, in GenreDefinitionsTest. broader gives a tag the tag of its broader genre (#1127), which a genre's panel names under its definition (\"Contemporary R&B is a style of R&B.\"): MusicBrainz's genre relationships (subgenre of, core data, CC0), read from each genre's MusicBrainz page on 2026-11-15, for a tag that is its MusicBrainz genre's own name, to a broader genre the list holds under MusicBrainz's name for it, the first listed where there are several (emo: alternative rock, not post-hardcore); design/design.md, A genre's broader genre.",
